# Denials this device is expected to take, in the format logd reads
# (scontext tcontext tclass tag). Listing one here grants nothing: it annotates the
# denial in bug reports so a genuinely new one stands out instead of being lost among
# the expected ones. The file ships as vendor/etc/selinux/selinux_denial_metadata.
#
# Every entry was checked one at a time against the stock policy for this device.
# The tags mean:
#   yogi/stock-denies      stock has no rule for it either, so stock takes the same denial
#   yogi/provider-absent   the service exists only if a Google app we do not ship registers it
#   aosp/neverallow        AOSP forbids granting it, so it cannot be fixed by a rule
#
# A fifth tag, for the one case that is not simply "stock has no rule": stock DOES grant
# platform_app find on gril_antenna_tuning_service, but the denial comes from platform_app_36.
# A versioned app domain carries platform_app_all, not platform_app, so stock's rule does not
# reach it either and stock takes the same denial. Do not "fix" this by comparing the
# unversioned name; that reads as a gap and is not one.
#   yogi/versioned-app-domain   stock has a rule, but on a name that does not cover this domain
#
# A limit worth knowing before trusting this file as a completeness check: logd can only
# annotate denials it receives over the audit netlink socket, which means kernel-object
# classes. service_manager decisions are made in userspace by servicemanager through
# libselinux, so the kernel emits no audit record and those denials reach logcat already
# formatted, under the SELinux tag, with no chance to be tagged. The service_manager entries
# below are therefore inert in practice and kept only so this file is a full record of what
# the device is expected to be refused.
#
# Denials from the adb shell reaching HALs are deliberately absent: they happen only
# while someone is running dumpsys, not during a boot, and listing them would hide a
# real shell-domain problem later.


